1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The dashboard exhibited a visible discrepancy where the highest return in FINAL TESTING
was approximately 148% (Node_718), whereas STRATEGY LABORATORY displayed approximately
342% (Node_240).

Our root cause investigation determined that:
(1) Strategy 240 (Return: 342.51%, PF: 3.326, 665 trades, M1) is indeed the highest
    in-sample return strategy in the database.
(2) Final Testing did not display Node_240 because of a duration filter field bug in
    the legacy post-evaluation filtering endpoint (_filter_persisted_strategies).
    Specifically, Final Testing initialized with a default filter of min_trade_duration_minutes=2.0
    (120 seconds). In legacy backtest records, avg_trade_duration_seconds was not serialized
    at the root of the metrics JSON dictionary (it was None). The filter evaluated
    m.get('avg_trade_duration_seconds', 0) < 120, which returned True (0 < 120), dropping
    Node_240 even though its actual sampled trades had an average duration of 28.26 minutes (1,695.4s).
(3) Node_718 (Return: 148.10%, PF: 2.545, 905 trades) was evaluated in a later run where
    avg_trade_duration_seconds=1154.5 was stored in the metrics root, allowing it to pass
    the 120s filter and appear as the highest return strategy in Final Testing.
(4) In addition, backtest stage selection was inconsistent: Strategy Laboratory prioritized
    detail backtest metrics over screening, whereas Final Testing selected the MAX(id)
    backtest without stage prioritization.
(5) In-sample backtest return and out-of-sample validation return were both labeled 'Return'
    generically without stage demarcation.

